Corners of the C library that CPython's test suite found, each as glibc
2.39 has it on Linux:
  - writev with no buffers writes nothing and returns 0, as a write of
    no bytes does, where an assertion ended the program; the descriptor
    is checked all the same (the system layer);
  - strsignal gives glibc's words for each signal, "Interrupt" for
    SIGINT where it gave "SIGINT", "Real-time signal N" for those from
    SIGRTMIN, and "Unknown signal N" for any other, and psignal with it;
  - fpathconf fails with EBADF for a descriptor that is not open, and
    pathconf with stat's errors for a path that is not there, where
    both answered for any;
  - setlogmask(0) leaves the mask as it is and returns it, as POSIX
    says, where it set an empty mask; syslog no longer drops messages
    of priority LOG_EMERG, which a test of the priority against the mask
    did, and openlog's LOG_PID, LOG_CONS and LOG_PERROR take effect, as
    a variable never set stood for them.
Meant for upstream.

